Reasoning: A website 403 with an intact public-read policy means the policy is being overridden, not evaluated. Confirm, then hunt the override.
<|call:curl_http|> {"method": "GET", "url": "http://docs-site-prod.s3-website-us-east-1.amazonaws.com/"}
System Response: HTTP 403 — AccessDenied
Recovery reasoning: Block Public Access is the standard override for public policies. Check the bucket.
<|call:aws_s3api_get_public_access_block|> {"bucket": "docs-site-prod"}
System Response: all four flags false — no bucket-level restrictions
Recovery reasoning: All-false looks exonerating, but BPA exists at two scopes and the effective setting is the most restrictive of the two; the hardening window was account-wide. Query the account scope.
<|call:aws_s3control_get_public_access_block|> {"account_id": "445120887733"}
System Response: all four flags true, including RestrictPublicBuckets — applied 16:41 UTC by stack org-hardening
Recovery reasoning: RestrictPublicBuckets makes S3 ignore the public bucket policy for anonymous callers — exactly website traffic, hence 403 at 16:41 sharp. Website endpoints cannot use CloudFront OAC directly, so the agreed fix: revert RestrictPublicBuckets, keep the other three flags, ticket the CloudFront migration.
<|call:aws_s3control_put_public_access_block|> {"account_id": "445120887733", "config": {"BlockPublicAcls": true, "IgnorePublicAcls": true, "BlockPublicPolicy": true, "RestrictPublicBuckets": false}}
System Response: account-level public access block updated 17:22 UTC
<|call:curl_http|> {"method": "GET", "url": "http://docs-site-prod.s3-website-us-east-1.amazonaws.com/"}
System Response: HTTP 200 — index.html, 12,408 bytes
Resolution: Site restored at 17:22. Root cause: account-level Block Public Access (RestrictPublicBuckets) from the hardening window overrode the bucket's public-read policy while bucket-level BPA read all-false; reverted with security sign-off.

Reasoning: A leaked key would leave fingerprints in CloudTrail data events. Query deletions first.
<|call:aws_cloudtrail_lookup|> {"event_name": "DeleteObject", "bucket": "ml-features-prod", "since": "-72h"}
System Response: 0 matching events; data events enabled and healthy (PutObject events present)
Recovery reasoning: Zero deletes with a healthy trail suggests a phantom actor — except lifecycle actions run inside S3 and never emit CloudTrail data events, and nightly batches right after 00:00 UTC are exactly the lifecycle cadence. Read the lifecycle config.
<|call:aws_s3api_get_bucket_lifecycle_configuration|> {"bucket": "ml-features-prod"}
System Response: Rule expire-tmp-artifacts: Enabled, Expiration Days=1, Filter {"Prefix": ""} — created 2026-06-29 by IaC
Recovery reasoning: An empty Prefix matches the whole bucket, expiring everything older than a day. On a versioned bucket expiration writes delete markers, not erasure — recoverable. Confirm via access logs, then fix.
<|call:query_s3_access_logs|> {"bucket": "ml-features-prod", "operation": "S3.EXPIRE.OBJECT", "window": "last-72h"}
System Response: S3.EXPIRE.OBJECT records: 48,112 + 48,041 + 48,053 = 144,206, all 00:00-00:19 UTC
Recovery reasoning: 412,304 - 144,206 = 268,098, matching the inventory. Fix the filter, sweep the markers.
<|call:aws_s3api_put_lifecycle|> {"bucket": "ml-features-prod", "rule": {"ID": "expire-tmp-artifacts", "Filter": {"Prefix": "tmp/"}, "Expiration": {"Days": 1}}}
System Response: lifecycle configuration replaced
<|call:run_delete_marker_sweep|> {"bucket": "ml-features-prod", "marker_created_since": "2026-06-30", "exclude_prefix": "tmp/"}
System Response: 144,206 delete markers removed; inventory count 412,298 (6 legit user deletes)
Resolution: 404 rate back to 0.02% baseline. Root cause: a lifecycle rule intended for tmp/ shipped with an empty Prefix filter, expiring the whole bucket nightly, invisible to CloudTrail; versioning made it markers — full recovery.

